/* =============================================================================
   DIAGNOSTIC — why does an option read "[missing role: R08]"?
   =============================================================================
   The app is not losing the option. It is showing you that a row in
   'RolePreference Eligibilities' points at a RoleKey that has no matching row
   in 'RolePreference Roles'. Before this fallback existed the row rendered
   blank, which is what UAT reported as "one of my preferences is blank".

   The user still sees seven rows and can still rank all seven — only the NAME
   is unresolvable. This is a data fix, not an app fix.

   Work through these in order. Put each on a temporary label or gallery on any
   screen, read it, then delete the control.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   1) Does R08 exist at all?
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Label → Text:

     "R08 -> [" & LookUp('RolePreference Roles', RoleKey = "R08").RoleName & "]"

   Empty brackets  = no such row. Go to (3).
   A name appears  = the row exists and the key matches exactly, so the problem
                     is elsewhere — check that your OnStart is the current one.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   2) List every role key you actually have
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Blank vertical gallery → Items:

     Sort('RolePreference Roles', RoleKey, SortOrder.Ascending)

   Row label → Text:

     "[" & ThisItem.RoleKey & "]  " & ThisItem.RoleName & "  active=" & ThisItem.Active

   Read the keys around R08. The square brackets matter — they make trailing
   spaces visible. Look for:
     - R08 simply absent (a row was dropped on import);
     - "R8" where you expected "R08" (leading zero lost — Excel does this when
       the column is typed as a number);
     - "R08 " with a trailing space (also common from Excel);
     - R08 present but active=false.
   Note that Active only affects colRoles, NOT the name lookup, so an inactive
   role would still resolve its name. If R08 shows here, the key does not match
   character for character.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   3) How big is the problem across ALL users?
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Do not fix this one person's row and assume you are done — the same import
   almost certainly affected others. Label → Text:

     CountRows(
         Filter(
             'RolePreference Eligibilities' As e,
             Not IsBlank(e.RoleKey) And IsBlank(LookUp('RolePreference Roles', RoleKey = e.RoleKey).RoleName)
         )
     ) & " eligibility rows point at a role that does not exist"

   And to see exactly which — blank vertical gallery → Items:

     Filter(
         'RolePreference Eligibilities' As e,
         Not IsBlank(e.RoleKey) And IsBlank(LookUp('RolePreference Roles', RoleKey = e.RoleKey).RoleName)
     )

   Row label → Text:

     ThisItem.EmployeeID & "  ->  [" & ThisItem.RoleKey & "]"

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   4) Fixing it
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Whichever it turns out to be, the fix is in Dataverse, not the app:

     - key missing from Roles   → add the role row with RoleKey = R08;
     - key mistyped on Roles    → correct RoleKey there;
     - key mistyped on          → correct RoleKey on the affected Eligibilities
       Eligibilities              rows;
     - trailing/leading spaces  → trim the key on whichever table carries it.

   Re-import both sheets as TEXT columns, not General/Number, or Excel will keep
   stripping leading zeros from R01..R09.

   Then run App.OnStart again. The "[missing role: ...]" text disappears on its
   own once the lookup resolves — nothing in the app needs changing.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   5) Anyone who already ranked a missing role
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Their saved Preferences row holds the RoleKey, not the name, so correcting
   the data resolves their ranking too — no resubmission needed. Check whether
   anyone did:

     CountRows(
         Filter(
             'RolePreference Preferences' As p,
             Not IsBlank(p.RoleKey) And IsBlank(LookUp('RolePreference Roles', RoleKey = p.RoleKey).RoleName)
         )
     ) & " saved rankings point at a role that does not exist"
   ============================================================================ */
